        title: "Administrative procedure, judicial review, and the limits of agency discretion",
        explanation:
          "Now turn to the second major point of exposure: how much discretion an agency has once it is actually interpreting and applying the law, and the red-tape-versus-control tension that exposure creates. The Administrative Procedure Act of 1946 is the foundational statute governing how federal agencies make rules and adjudicate cases, requiring notice-and-comment rulemaking for most significant regulations, publishing a proposed rule, accepting public comment, and responding to significant objections, before a final rule takes effect, and providing for judicial review of agency action that is arbitrary, capricious, or contrary to law [5]. For decades, courts applied Chevron deference, from Chevron v. NRDC in 1984, giving agencies broad latitude to interpret ambiguous statutes they administer, on the theory that technical expertise and political accountability both sat with the agency rather than the court. The Supreme Court's 2024 decision in Loper Bright Enterprises v. Raimondo overturned Chevron, holding that courts must exercise independent judgment on questions of statutory interpretation rather than defer to an agency's reading, a genuinely major shift in how much latitude agencies now have to fill gaps in ambiguous statutes [6]. The notice-and-comment process itself is the main structural check on arbitrary discretion short of litigation, forcing an agency to justify a rule's reasoning against the public record it generated.",
