          "Now turn to the institution built specifically to check whether all of the above is actually working as claimed. The Government Accountability Office, led by the Comptroller General and formally an arm of the legislative branch rather than the executive it audits, conducts financial audits, program evaluations, and the bid-protest reviews covered earlier in this lecture, publishing findings and recommendations that agencies are not legally compelled to adopt but face sustained public and congressional pressure to address [7]. GAO's independence is structural, not merely cultural: the Comptroller General serves a fifteen-year term specifically to outlast any single administration or Congress, and GAO's funding and reporting lines run through the legislative branch precisely so its findings are not subject to control by the executive agencies it reviews. GAO also maintains a published High-Risk List, agencies or programs it judges to be at serious risk of waste, fraud, abuse, or mismanagement, a standing public record that tracks whether a flagged problem is actually being fixed or merely acknowledged, which creates a form of accountability that persists across multiple budget cycles and multiple administrations rather than fading after a single news cycle.",
