          "Now turn to how waste, fraud, and abuse actually get discovered in the first place, often from inside the organization rather than from an outside auditor. The Inspector General Act of 1978 created independent inspectors general within most major federal agencies, offices positioned to investigate waste, fraud, and abuse with a reporting line that runs both to the agency head and directly to Congress, a dual-reporting structure specifically designed so an inspector general's findings cannot simply be buried by the agency being investigated. The Whistleblower Protection Act of 1989, along with later amendments, protects federal employees from retaliation for disclosing what they reasonably believe is illegal conduct, gross mismanagement, or a substantial danger to public health or safety, while the civil False Claims Act, dating to the Civil War era and significantly strengthened since, allows private citizens to file qui tam suits on the government's behalf against contractors who defraud federal programs, sharing in any resulting financial recovery as an incentive to come forward [8]. Together these mechanisms create overlapping, partially redundant channels, internal IG referral, formal whistleblower complaint, and qui tam litigation, precisely because any single channel can fail or be captured.",
