          "Now turn to the transparency obligation that runs alongside all of this, and the genuine tension it creates with candid internal deliberation. The Freedom of Information Act of 1966 gives the public a legally enforceable right to request records from federal agencies, with agencies required to respond within statutory deadlines and to justify any withheld material under one of nine specific statutory exemptions, rather than simply declining to answer [9]. Exemption 5 is the one most relevant to this lecture's accountability theme: it protects the deliberative-process privilege, internal agency memos, draft analyses, and pre-decisional recommendations, on the theory that officials need genuine space to debate options candidly, including bad ones, without every draft becoming a permanent public record that chills frank internal advice. The privilege is not absolute: it only covers material that is both pre-decisional, created before a final decision, and deliberative, reflecting the give-and-take of policy formulation, which means a final decision memo, or an internal document simply explaining an already-made decision, is not protected merely because it originated inside the agency, and courts regularly order disclosure when an agency has stretched the exemption past that boundary.",
