          "Now turn to the structural risk that comes specifically from regulating an industry closely enough to need its expertise, and sometimes its people. Independent regulatory commissions, the Securities and Exchange Commission, the Federal Communications Commission, and others, are deliberately structured with multi-member, bipartisan boards and fixed, staggered terms specifically to insulate technical regulatory judgment from being swept out wholesale by every change in administration, mirroring the same logic behind the Comptroller General's fifteen-year term covered earlier in this lecture. The specific risk this structure cannot fully eliminate is regulatory capture, the theory, most associated with economist George Stigler, that over time a regulator's decisions can come to serve the regulated industry's interests rather than the public's, often not through corruption but through the ordinary mechanics of who the regulator talks to daily, whose technical expertise it depends on, and who it expects to work for after leaving government [10]. The revolving door, movement of regulatory staff into the industry they regulated, and sometimes back again, is capture's most visible symptom, governed in part by the Lobbying Disclosure Act and by post-employment restrictions on former officials lobbying their own former agency, though those restrictions are narrower and more easily navigated than most of the public assumes.",
