    connections:
      "Ten ideas, one recurring test: who is actually accountable for this outcome, and through what mechanism would anyone find out if they weren't. The politics-administration dichotomy and its principal-agent problem are the foundational tension every other concept in this lecture is a response to, and the merit-based civil service is the personnel system built to make that dichotomy real rather than aspirational, protecting administration from patronage so it can actually be held to professional rather than partisan standards. Competitive procurement and administrative-procedure judicial review are the two places public money and public power are most directly exposed, and performance measurement is how an agency's results get tracked between the moments those exposures are tested, with Goodhart's law as the standing warning that the measurement itself can be captured even when nothing else is. GAO's independent audit function and the inspector-general/whistleblower system are the two main channels, external and internal, through which failure actually gets discovered, while FOIA and the deliberative-process privilege set the public's right to see the resulting record against officials' genuine need for candid internal deliberation. Regulatory capture and the revolving door are the structural risk of working closely enough with an industry to regulate it well, and agency-level redress and digital-government reform close the loop by catching, at the level of the individual citizen and the individual technology program, whatever the larger structures upstream still missed. As you follow any real administrative controversy from here forward, try asking which of these ten mechanisms should have caught the problem, and whether it actually did, or whether, like the VA wait-time data or the original HealthCare.gov launch, the failure reached the public before any of the mechanisms built to catch it actually worked.",
